<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islamisation</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Islamisation refers to the process by which societies or rulers adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Islamic institutions, through conversion, conquest, trade or reform. It occurred in different ways across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>'s conquests and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> promoted it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a> was founded on it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>'s Mongol rulers gradually converted.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In West Africa, the southern branch under Abu Bakr ibn Umar helped spread Maliki Islam through the Sahel, contributing to the <b>Islamisation</b> of the western Sudan and shaping the religious character of the later Mali and Songhai empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultanate also laid foundations for the later <b>Islamisation</b> of eastern Bengal, which historians such as Richard Eaton connect with land settlement on the forest frontier, a process that accelerated under the Mughals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…khanate's legal framework drew on the Yasa, the Mongol customary law attributed to Genghis Khan, but the progressive <b>Islamisation</b> of the western khanate brought in elements of Islamic law that conflicted with the Yasa's provisions, and this was one of the central political tensions of the fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Solong's work was political and religious: he united the Fur and neighbouring groups under a single Keira dynasty and began the <b>Islamisation</b> of the royal court, which placed the sultanate within the wider network of Sahelian Islamic states stretching from Bornu to Sennar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kara-Khanids were the first Turkic dynasty to make Islam the religion of their state, and their conversion opened the way for the <b>Islamisation</b> of the Turkic peoples of Central Asia, from whom later Muslim Turkic dynasties such as the Seljuks emerged.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The gradual <b>Islamisation</b> of coastal regions eroded the ideological foundations of the Hindu-Buddhist court, and many of its nobles, priests and artists are traditionally said to have withdrawn to Bali.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
